WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.560
Climbers attempting to summit Mount Everest
and stand on the top of the world have their

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.990
work cut out from them.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:11.230
Dangers are everywhere on the long climb,
there’s risk of extreme altitude sickness,

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:13.599
unpredictable winds, and freezing weather.

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:19.269
There’s also the threat of avalanches - great,
unstoppable tidal waves of snow that can bury

00:00:19.269 --> 00:00:24.019
a person alive in seconds or strand them,
leaving them to freeze to death, cold and

00:00:24.019 --> 00:00:25.019
alone.

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:30.039
It’s a sad reality that not everyone that
visits Everest comes back down again, and

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:35.829
rarely does a climbing season go by that the
mountain doesn’t claim yet another victim.

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:41.280
Situated on the border between China and Nepal,
in a sub-range of the Himalayas of Tibet,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.329
Everest is one of the Seven Wonders of the
Natural World.

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:50.289
With an elevation of 29,031 feet - or 8,848
meters.

00:00:50.289 --> 00:00:54.420
Mount Everest has attracted many experienced
mountain climbers over the decades.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:00.110
But even for the most experienced mountaineers,
the ascension up its snowy slopes is far from

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:01.110
easy.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:05.000
Deaths occurring on Mount Everest are nothing
new and hundreds of people are believed to

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.050
have died attempting to ascend the treacherous
peak.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.630
Sadly, due to the risks involved with carrying
heavy loads down off the mountain, many of

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:17.720
those who die on Everest are doomed to stay
there forever, their frozen bodies serving

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.270
as their own grave markers.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:24.590
It’s difficult enough to move your own body
up and down Everest, and in many situations,

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:27.960
bringing a body back down is simply impossible.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.040
The bodies of these lost souls are scattered
all across, and sometimes underneath, the

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.430
surface of Mount Everest.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:39.590
But some of those bodies are different from
the others, and have a special name within

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.399
the files of the SCP-Foundation.

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:45.160
They call them SCP-5140.

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:51.439
SCP-5140 is the designation given to a number
of frozen bodies which are found all across

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:52.439
Everest.

00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:57.850
It is unclear exactly how many instances of
SCP-5140 there are, though some estimates

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:01.550
have placed the number at somewhere between
one and two hundred.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:07.380
At present, it is unknown if all the instances
of SCP-5140 are dead bodies belonging to climbers

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.950
who met tragedy on the tallest mountain in
the world, or if some of them are something

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.270
else entirely.

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:19.570
But while their origin is unclear, if you
come in contact with a 5140 instance, there

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:24.980
won’t be any doubt that it’s different
from the normal body of a dead climber.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:30.300
That’s because SCP-5140 corpses are a lot
like vampires in some ways.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:35.160
That isn’t to say that they actually are
vampires, they certainly are not undead and

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.000
cannot function after death.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.730
But, much like a vampire traditionally drains
blood to survive, these bodies drain heat.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:48.920
SCP-5140 corpses are ectoentropic, meaning
that they do not adhere to the normal laws

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.590
of thermodynamics.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:55.941
Whenever these cadavers are exposed to sources
of heat, including human body heat, the corpse

00:02:55.941 --> 00:02:59.760
will instantly drain the source of any and
all thermal energy.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.650
However, the temperature of these SCP-5140
bodies remains the same, regardless of how

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.900
much heat they are able to drain, staying
at a consistent fifty degrees Fahrenheit,

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.630
or ten degrees Celsius.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:18.240
Should any living person come into direct
contact with one of the SCP-5140 corpses,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.610
the body will absorb their natural body heat,
bringing their victim’s own temperature

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:27.030
down to ten degrees celsius, and causing them
to die.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:32.950
And once they have died, their body then becomes
a new instance of SCP-5140.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:37.990
As a quick side note, this might feel vaguely
reminiscent of another anomaly, which features

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:44.640
seemingly inert dead bodies that are hazardous
to anyone around them: SCP-2316.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.630
This anomaly is a powerful memetic hazard
consisting of a large number of drowned human

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:50.630
corpses.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:55.380
These corpses are extremely hazardous to approach
at even a considerable distance, as the memetic

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.220
hazard inherent to the anomaly causes those
affected to recognize the bodies in the water

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.750
as people they know - particularly from their
childhood.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:09.800
Like a siren’s call, victims are lured into
the water, believing that the familiar bodies

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.430
are calling to them.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:16.709
If the victims aren’t stopped, quarantined,
and given amnestic treatment, they are essentially

00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:18.370
considered lost.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:22.920
They will walk into the water, and be absorbed
into the collective, becoming just another

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:24.570
body floating in the water.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:28.700
While the Foundation doesn’t currently believe
that there is any official connection between

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:35.410
the two anomalies, the similarities are undeniable,
and worth taking into consideration.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:36.710
Moving on.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:41.630
At present, it is unclear whether SCP-5140
corpses are caused by some sort of infection

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:45.840
or parasite that spreads among the bodies
uncovered at Mount Everest.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:50.370
If this is the case, the Foundation has yet
to uncover the “patient zero”, the root

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.800
cause of these infected bodies.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:57.550
There is also little to determine why a deceased
body requires the absorption of heat, as well

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:02.440
as how it achieves this and also keeps its
own temperature low but not frozen.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:07.540
Given that interacting with an instance of
SCP-5140 can be fatal, it does seem that these

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:13.830
anomalous properties can be passed through
contact and exposure, like a disease or parasite.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:18.420
But there have been no observed anomalous
effects among any living person visiting Mount

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:19.460
Everest.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:25.350
There is a possibility that the SCP-5140 corpses
have developed some form of posthumous mutation

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:29.090
that has come as a result of being killed
during an Everest avalanche and buried in

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:33.130
the snow, their bodies starved of heat after
death.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:38.910
After all, Everest is known for its cold temperatures,
occasionally reaching as low as one degree

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.290
Fahrenheit, or minus seventeen degrees Celsius.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:46.430
Not to mention the difference in air pressure
at such high altitudes, as well as frequent

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.800
high speed freezing cold winds.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.260
At the summit of Mount Everest, wind speeds
can reach up to 280 kilometers per hour.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:06:00.040
That is equivalent to 175 miles per hour,
and high speed winds have even been known

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.730
to catch mountain climbers off guard and send
them plummeting to their deaths.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:08.970
Perhaps the combination of these conditions;
the freezing temperature, the air pressure

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:13.130
and the winds, have triggered some sort of
change in a number of the bodies unfortunate

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:16.550
enough to never leave the snow-covered slopes
of Mount Everest.

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:22.700
Either that, or these abandoned corpses left
in such extreme cold could be the perfect

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:23.700
home.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:28.850
Maybe some form of unknown, and so far undetected
parasite is capable of inhabiting these frozen

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:34.310
bodies, and can spread to other living hosts
by draining them of their body heat.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:38.620
Stranger things have certainly happened at
the SCP Foundation, and that could possibly

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:45.120
explain why anyone interacting with an SCP-5140
corpse meets the same grisly fate.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.060
Regardless of what causes these abnormalities,
or why the SCP 5140 bodies have only been

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.970
uncovered at Mount Everest, close contact
with the corpses often results in casualties

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:55.970
and fatalities.

00:06:55.970 --> 00:07:01.510
Naturally, this is a cost the SCP Foundation
likes to avoid as often as it can.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:07.240
The nasty habit SCP-5140 corpses have of draining
a person’s body heat makes it impractical

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.050
to bring them back to a Foundation research
facility for testing - as does the fact they’re

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:14.180
on the tallest mountain in the world.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:19.590
Instead, MTF-Delta-14 have been tasked with
keeping these heat-thirsty cadavers contained

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.020
to Mount Everest.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:25.389
Mobile Task Force Delta Fourteen, also known
under the code name “Winter Wonderland”,

00:07:25.389 --> 00:07:29.880
is formed of a group of particularly adept
mountaineers, and experts in understanding

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:35.120
and adapting to particularly brutal terrain
without drawing attention to themselves.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:40.010
These covert operatives are tasked with periodically
climbing Mount Everest, and keeping SCP-5140

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:41.280
contained.

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.690
It is this team’s job to locate any new
instances of SCP-5140 bodies and bury them

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:47.919
in the snow.

00:07:47.919 --> 00:07:52.590
In doing so, the goal is to prevent any unwitting
mountain climbers from stumbling upon one

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:56.600
of these bodies and potentially becoming a
new instance themselves.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:01.370
Meanwhile, the Foundation is pushing for both
Nepal and Tibet to tighten restrictions on

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:02.850
climbing permits.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:07.330
But of course, the money that Mount Everest
tourism brings to these two countries has

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:09.430
likely impeded this.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:14.260
According to the Foundation’s file on SCP-5140,
an incident occurred during one of Mobile

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:17.980
Task Force Delta Fourteen’s annual Everest
expeditions.

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:22.880
Members of MTF Deta-14 had been traversing
towards the Everest summit, encountering more

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:27.530
and more of the SCP-5140 bodies as they made
their way up the mountain.

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:31.820
Just as they were instructed to do, the team
buried each of the frozen corpses they came

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:33.870
across under a blanket of the snow.

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:37.900
The Delta Fourteens noticed though, that there
had been a much higher number of the frosty

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:42.610
cadavers on this mission, speculating that
perhaps Mount Everest’s high winds had blown

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:46.630
the snow off ones they had already buried
on previous missions.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:51.690
One of the Delta Fourteens, a man named Charlie
Smith, was in correspondence with his colleagues

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:52.750
at their basecamp.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:56.780
Charlie reported that one of his fellow Winter
Wonderlanders, Patrick, had touched one of

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:01.779
the SCP 5140 corpses when the team reached
the summit, and that he was now buried according

00:09:01.779 --> 00:09:06.860
to protocols after becoming a 5140 instance
himself.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:12.430
As they began their descent from atop Everest,
Charlie sent an urgent communication to basecamp,

00:09:12.430 --> 00:09:17.760
reporting that he’d uncovered three more
SCP 5140 bodies at an abandoned campsite.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.589
Except these three instances were different
from any he had encountered before.

00:09:22.589 --> 00:09:26.350
These corpses were somehow still moving.

00:09:26.350 --> 00:09:30.850
Attempting to hurry down the mountain, Charlie
reported that a mass of SCP-5140 corpses were

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:35.610
blocking his path, and draining another of
his squad mates, Arnold, of his body heat

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:37.490
to make him one of them.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:41.720
Arnold had been carrying the team’s provisions,
including their oxygen reserves to combat

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:45.290
the lack of breathable air at Mount Everest’s
high altitude.

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:50.040
Charlie tried in vain to use his flare gun
to clear a path, hoping to distract the now-walking

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:53.730
cadavers with a heat source, but it had no
effect.

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:57.740
His final message to base camp warned the
rest of Delta Fourteen to not come to the

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:02.350
summit of Everest, with Charlie frantically
urging them to not let climbers anywhere near

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:03.350
it.

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:06.980
His final message stated simply that“The
Mountain is danger”.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:12.350
A rescue division of Mobile Task Force Delta
Fourteen arrived just under two hours later,

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:15.930
tracking Charlie’s last known location via
a GPS beacon.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:21.460
Strangely, despite the frantic messages from
Charlie, there were no signs of any SCP-5140

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:22.820
instances.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:27.040
None of the piles of corpses he had warned
them about could be seen above the snow, and

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:29.000
certainly none of them were moving.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:32.070
It wasn’t long before the rescue team found
Charlie though.

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:37.450
He was dead but not drained of heat or turned
into another instance of SCP-5140.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:42.899
No, sadly after bringing their fallen comrade
back to base, an autopsy revealed that Charlie

00:10:42.899 --> 00:10:47.750
had likely died the same way so many other
climbers on Everest had, he had suffocated

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:49.580
from a lack of oxygen.

00:10:49.580 --> 00:10:53.360
Further examination of his gear revealed that
Charlie Charlie’s oxygen intake valve had

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:57.180
been damaged earlier while the squad had been
climbing to the summit of Mount Everest.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:01.560
His teammates, Arnold Hillary and Patrick
Edmund were also discovered on the mountain,

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:05.690
but only Patrick had been turned into an SCP-5140
instance.

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:09.330
Arnold had simply died from exposure, just
like Charlie.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:14.060
The rescue team concluded that, due to lack
of adequate oxygen, Charlie Smith had hallucinated

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:19.960
the sight of SCP-5140 corpses moving and blocking
his path, spending his final moments in terror

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:22.770
as the mountain’s conditions slowly killed
him.

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:29.030
The Foundation is still unsure exactly what
causes bodies to become instances of SCP-5140,

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:33.520
or even how many of them are on Everest, but
a major and troubling development has recently

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:34.780
been made.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:38.750
After an increase in global temperatures led
to a large avalanche that resulted in a large

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:43.370
portion of the mountain that had previously
been covered in thick snow being exposed,

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:47.060
the Foundation sent planes that were equipped
with thermal imaging equipment to scan the

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:48.060
mountain.

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:51.800
What they found was as confusing as it was
terrifying.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:56.890
The SCP-5140 corpses aren’t just spread
across the surface of Mount Everest, they

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:59.240
are much of Everest.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:04.580
The mountain itself is a huge pile of corpses,
just waiting to drain the life from anyone

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:08.910
who attempts to scale it and turn them into
a permanent part of the peak.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:12.860
So if you were planning a trip to the Himalayas,
hoping to scratch “climb Mount Everest”

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:16.140
off of your bucket list, maybe give it a pass.

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:20.190
Otherwise you might find that the climb is
even colder than you were expecting.

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:27.270
Now check out “SCP-1861 - The Crew of the
HMS Wintersheimer” and “SCP-3082 - Neverland's

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:31.590
Lost Boys and Girls” for more mind-boggling
mysteries from the SCP Foundation!

